      "primary_source": "official IFAAMAS AAMAS 2026 proceedings paper PDF",
      "abstract_evidence_excerpt": "Serving large language models (LLMs) poses major challenges for cluster schedulers due to highly variable request sizes, strict latency constraints, and dynamic load fluctuations. Existing approaches either optimize local routing or rely on reactive migration, but they often lack a unified design that combines global awareness with predictive foresight. This work presents GAPS, a Globally-Aware Predictive Scheduler for LLM inference clusters. GAPS integrates online monitoring with lightweight, bias-aware prediction to anticipate queueing delays, and complements this with selective request rebalancing to alleviate transient hotspots. The design jointly prevents tail amplification and mitigates imbalance with minimal migration overhead. GAPS is implemented in a discrete-event simulator and evaluated on both code-oriented and conversational workloads. Experimental results show that GAPS reduces P99 latency by up to 25% and lowers SLO violation rates by 10–15% compared with state-of-the-art baselines. Scalability experiments confirm stable improvements as cluster size grows, prediction robustness experiments demonstrate resilience under 30–40% error, and ablation studies highlight the necessity of combining prediction, rebalancing, and global awareness. Complexity analysis further shows that GAPS operates in polynomial time, ensuring scalability without prohibitive scheduling overhead. Overall, the findings indicate that global awareness and predictive scheduling are key to robust and efficient LLM inference, offering a practical design for next-generation AI service clusters.",
      "abstract_evidence_sha256": "a411979aaf64c82c786a2ffe2013eae35269c7892e4843432da220c2afefea6a",
      "source_version": "AAMAS 2026",
      "source_published": null,
      "source_updated": null,
      "body_review": {
        "full_text_read": true,
        "full_text_url": "https://ifaamas.org/Proceedings/aamas2026/pdfs/YPTR3460.pdf",
        "sections_read": [
          "3 Problem Formulation (3.1 System Model)",
          "4 Method (4.1 Method Overview; 4.2 Global Awareness Layer; 4.3 Scheduling and Rebalancing; 4.4 Bias-Aware SLO Correction)",
          "5 Experiments (5.1 Experimental Setup; 5.2 Performance and Scalability; 5.3 Ablation Study; 5.4 Prediction Robustness)",
          "6 Conclusion"
        ],
        "identity_confirmed": true,
        "finding": "GAPS combines global GPU queue/utilization monitoring, lightweight per-request service-time prediction with online bias calibration, and selective request rebalancing. Section 5 evaluates it in a discrete-event simulator on Azure-derived code and conversational traces against Splitwise, Llumnix, and FlashGen; it reports up to 25% lower P99 latency and 10–15 percentage-point lower SLO violation rates. Section 6 says production deployment is future work, so results are not production evidence.",
        "body_reviewed_at": "2026-10-14"
      }
